---
import ProjectPage from '../components/ProjectPage.astro';
---

<ProjectPage
	title="Cube World Aero"
	subtitle="A cube landscape built from scratch in WebGL."
	image="aeroCubeWorld.png"
	alt="Cube World Aero: a green cube landscape with a city, windmills and floating clouds under a blue sky"
	url="https://maya2234.github.io/AeroCubeWorld/"
	cta="Open the scene"
	tags={['WebGL', 'JavaScript', 'Shaders']}
>
	<p>
		A small WebGL scene written without a 3D library: the geometry, matrices and shading all go straight
		through the raw WebGL API, wrapped in a glossy Frutiger Aero interface. Tap to blow bubbles and drag
		to look around.
	</p>

	<Fragment slot="details">
		<div>
			<h2>How it is built</h2>
			<p>
				One cube is copied across a grid, moved to its cell and stretched in height, with a colour on each
				corner. Perspective and look-at matrices place the camera, and the fragment shader blends the corner
				colours and lights every face.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div>
			<h2>Levels</h2>
			<ol>
				<li>Heights and per-vertex colour.</li>
				<li>Heights and colours sampled from a height map and a colour map.</li>
				<li>Clouds, butterflies and bubbles moving along cubic Bézier curves, each with its own model matrix.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>
		<div>
			<h2>Performance</h2>
			<p>
				The whole landscape is one buffer drawn in a single call, so it stays smooth up to tens of thousands
				of cubes. A live readout shows the grid size, build time and frame rate.
			</p>
		</div>
	</Fragment>
</ProjectPage>
